From: fish <lunarisfelix02@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 06:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] op7t-0155: USB RNDIS (sweetusb=3) so Windows can reach the phone without a driver

The USB gadget is CDC ECM (sweetusb=2), which Linux and macOS drive in the
box but Windows does not. sweetusb=3 runs the same data path as RNDIS, which
Windows has built in ("Remote NDIS Compatible Device"):

 - descriptors: device class 0xef/2/1 with an IAD, comm interface 0xe0/1/3
   (what Windows matches), interrupt 0x82, data interface with bulk 0x81/0x01.
   PID 0x0002 (ECM stays 0x0001) so a Windows that cached a failed
   1209:0001 install starts clean.
 - ep0: SEND_ENCAPSULATED_COMMAND now has an OUT data stage (up to 448
   bytes); GET_ENCAPSULATED_RESPONSE answers it; the interrupt endpoint
   carries an 8-byte RESPONSE_AVAILABLE per response.
 - messages: INITIALIZE, QUERY (the OIDs Windows asks for: addresses, link
   speed, media state, statistics, supported list), SET (packet filter,
   lookahead, multicast list, power), RESET, KEEPALIVE, HALT.
 - data: every bulk transfer is one RNDIS_PACKET_MSG (44-byte header, then
   the Ethernet frame); the 512-byte pad byte of ECM goes inside the message.
 - the link (frames to the host, live log, SIOCGIFMEDIA) comes up when the
   host sets a non-zero packet filter, goes down on a bus reset.
 - ECM (sweetusb=2) behaves exactly as before.

Same ue0 and 192.168.7.2/24. Host: give the new adapter 192.168.7.1/24,
then ssh root@192.168.7.2. The phone must not be bound in usbipd (a bound
device gets usbipd's driver, not RNDIS).

Apply after 0154.
---
